    <subsection xml:id="sec-assessment-of-mathematical-engagement">
      <title>Assessment of Mathematical Engagement</title>

      <p>
        Your level of effort and engagement will be assessed through your participation in class and completion of a variety of homework assignments.
        <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Homework</title>
        <p>
Homework problems will be assigned for each topic. 1 homework assignment
will be dropped.
</p></li>

        <li>
          <title>Reflection Posts</title>
          <p>
Every week either I will post a prompt for you to react or you would need to reflect on
what was happening in class that week: like the pace, difficulty of new topic, etc. They
will count towards your participation part of the grade.
</p></li>
        <li>
          <title>In-Class Participation</title>
          <p>
Throughout the semester, we will engage in active learning activities such as gallery walks,
group problem-solving, and practice sessions during class. These activities are designed
to help you develop mathematical reasoning, communicate your ideas, and learn from
your peers. Your participation may include contributing to group discussions, working
through problems collaboratively, and sharing solutions or strategies. Consistent, meaningful
engagement in these activities will count toward the participation portion of your
grade.
</p></li>
        <li>
          <title>Midterms and Final</title>
          <p>
The exams are cumulative in a sense that you will need to know some previous material
in order to successfully solve the problems. The exam dates are already scheduled, so
please mark your calendars now (midterms in our classroom at the usual meeting time).
The exams are open book. If you have special accommodations please talk to me as soon
as you can.</p></li>
        <li>
          <title>MATLAB Computational Labs</title>
          <p>
The goals of these labs are to familiarize students with MatLab (which, as a UHart student,
you can get for free here:
https://www.hartford.edu/academics/resources/matlab/MATLAB.html)
and to use the concepts from differential equations and linear algebra to solve applied
problems. All projects must be submitted through blackboard using the Assignment tool.
Do NOT email the projects to me! The grade will consist of 20 points: 10 "for correctness"
and 10 "for presentation".
        </p>
        </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsection>

